hostname aggregator
!
ip routing
!
interface Loopback0
   ip address 10.0.0.2/32
!
interface Ethernet1
   description to originator:eth1
   no switchport
   ip address 10.1.12.2/30
!
interface Ethernet2
   description to receiver:eth1
   no switchport
   ip address 10.1.23.1/30
!
! ============================================================
! YOUR TASKS ON AGGREGATOR
! ============================================================
!
! TASK 1 — Base BGP
!   configure
!   router bgp 65002
!      bgp router-id 10.0.0.2
!      neighbor 10.1.12.1 remote-as 65001
!      neighbor 10.1.23.2 remote-as 65003
!      !
!      address-family ipv4
!         neighbor 10.1.12.1 activate
!         neighbor 10.1.23.2 activate
!         network 10.0.0.2/32
!      !
!
!   Verify: receiver should see all four /24s and the loopbacks.
!
! TASK 2 — Create a basic aggregate (summary + specifics)
!   The aggregate-address command creates a summary route.
!   It ONLY activates when at least one more-specific is present in the BGP table.
!
!   router bgp 65002
!      address-family ipv4
!         aggregate-address 10.1.0.0/21
!      !
!
!   Verify on receiver: show bgp ipv4 unicast
!   Expected: BOTH 10.1.0.0/21 (aggregate) AND the four /24s (specifics).
!   The aggregate has the "atomic-aggregate" attribute set by default.
!   The aggregate AS-path is empty (appears as locally originated).
!
!   Detail: show bgp ipv4 unicast 10.1.0.0/21
!   Look for: "Atomic Aggregate" in the output.
!
! TASK 3 — summary-only: suppress the component specifics
!   aggregate-address 10.1.0.0/21 summary-only
!
!   Verify on receiver: show bgp ipv4 unicast
!   Expected: ONLY 10.1.0.0/21 — the four /24s are SUPPRESSED.
!
!   Important: "suppressed" means aggregator still has them locally
!   (show bgp ipv4 unicast on aggregator shows them with 's' flag)
!   but they are NOT advertised to receiver.
!
! TASK 4 — as-set: preserve AS-path information in the aggregate
!   aggregate-address 10.1.0.0/21 summary-only as-set
!
!   NOTE: cEOS 4.35.2F does not implement as-set per RFC 4271.
!   Both "summary-only" and "summary-only as-set" produce identical wire output:
!   AS_SEQUENCE 65002 65001, no atomic-aggregate attribute.
!   In a standards-compliant implementation, as-set would produce AS_SET {65001}
!   and suppress the atomic-aggregate attribute.
!
! TASK 5 — Observe aggregate behavior when a component is withdrawn
!   While aggregate is active, have originator remove one component.
!   On originator:
!     router bgp 65001
!        address-family ipv4
!           no network 10.1.4.0/24
!
!   Question: Does the aggregate 10.1.0.0/21 stay active on aggregator?
!   Answer: YES — as long as at least ONE component is in the BGP table,
!           the aggregate remains active.
!
!   Remove ALL four components on originator — then the aggregate withdraws.
!
! TASK 6 — suppress-map: selectively suppress only some components
!   Instead of suppressing ALL with summary-only, suppress only one specific route
!   while letting others still be advertised:
!
!   NOTE: EOS uses "summary-only match-map" instead of "suppress-map".
!   Routes that MATCH the map are suppressed; routes that do NOT match pass through.
!
!   ip prefix-list SUPPRESS-4 seq 5 permit 10.1.4.0/24
!
!   route-map SELECTIVE-SUPPRESS permit 10
!      match ip address prefix-list SUPPRESS-4
!
!   router bgp 65002
!      address-family ipv4
!         aggregate-address 10.1.0.0/21 summary-only match-map SELECTIVE-SUPPRESS
!      !
!
!   Result:
!   - Aggregate 10.1.0.0/21 is advertised
!   - 10.1.1.0/24, 10.1.2.0/24, 10.1.3.0/24 are still advertised (not suppressed)
!   - 10.1.4.0/24 is suppressed
! ============================================================
end
